; The "Smoothing (WGSL conventions)" trio. Two things here are worth a
; fixture rather than a comment.
;
; `step` (forms 0/1) is `x < edge ? 0 : 1`, so the boundary belongs to the
; upper branch: (step 0.5 0.5) is 1, and the largest double below 0.5 is
; still 0. Which side the equality falls on is the entire content of the
; function; nothing else about it can be got wrong.
;
; `smoothstep` with low == high (forms 5/6/7) is what WGSL calls
; indeterminate, and `applySmoothstep` deliberately does not special-case
; it — it lets (x − low) / (high − low) go non-finite and leans on the
; clamp. The result is NOT the 0.0 the source comment claims: the clamp is
; `@max(lo, @min(hi, v))`, and IEEE min/max return the non-NaN operand, so
; the 0/0 NaN at x == low clamps to 1.0, not 0.0. Below low the quotient is
; −inf and clamps to 0; above it is +inf and clamps to 1. So the degenerate
; edge is a step function whose rising edge sits *at* low — well-defined,
; reproducible on every host, and not what the comment beside it says.
; Pinning it here means a future "cleanup" that special-cases low == high
; has to change this file on purpose.
;
; Forms 2/3/4 are the ordinary reading, including the two clamped tails.
(step 0.5 0.5)
(step 0.5 0.49999999999999994)
(smoothstep 0.0 1.0 0.5)
(smoothstep 0.0 1.0 -1.0)
(smoothstep 0.0 1.0 2.0)
(smoothstep 1.0 1.0 1.0)
(smoothstep 1.0 1.0 0.0)
(smoothstep 1.0 1.0 2.0)
(saturate -0.5)
(saturate 0.25)
(saturate 1.5)
